`@@oauth-authorize` now sends an unauthenticated end user to log in, instead of refusing the request. It previously answered the client with `error=login_required`, which broke the flow outright — a relying party would redirect a browser to the authorization server and get it straight back, having never shown a login page — and misread the specification besides: `login_required` is what an authorization server returns when the client sent `prompt=none`, so the endpoint behaved as though every request forbade interaction. The fix is to stop opting out rather than to add a redirect: the view raises `Unauthorized` and Plone's own challenge machinery takes over, which matters because the stock challenge carries the query string into `came_from` and reduces it to a local URL — and an authorization request *is* its query string, while sanitising a return address is precisely the thing not to implement twice. `prompt=none` is now honoured, so `login_required` stays reachable for the client that actually asked not to interact, and an unconsented request under it answers `consent_required` rather than rendering a form the client forbade.

Fixed three bugs in the optional challenge plugin, which is the switch a site acting as an authorization server would reach for first. It dropped the query string, building `came_from` from `ACTUAL_URL` alone — enough for an ordinary page, fatal for an authorization request, which would resume with no client, no redirect URI and no PKCE challenge. It neither quoted nor sanitised `came_from`, so the first `&` of a query string was read as another parameter of the login URL, and an absolute URL made the login form an open redirect. And it pointed at `@@identity-login`, which is not a registered view anywhere; it now points at `/login`, which the Volto add-on overrides with the provider picker and which a Classic site serves with Plone's own form. The plugin is opt-in and off by default, so no working site was affected. @ericof
